# Kernel messages go to the second virtual console (Alt+F2 shows it, Alt+F1
# returns), not over the panel's shell: console=tty0 otherwise paints every
# error at the console loglevel onto tty1 -- a C6 restart writes dozens of
# esp32_sdio lines across whatever is on the screen.  The serial console
# (ttyGS1), the ramoops console record and dmesg still get every message.
# setlogcons opens /dev/tty2 itself, which allocates the VT; devtmpfs is
# mounted by the kernel, so this runs before rcS.
::sysinit:/usr/sbin/setlogcons 2
# The kernel's keymap switches consoles with the LEFT Alt only (Alt+F1/F2,
# Alt+Left/Right).  A tablet-case Bluetooth keyboard in its Windows mode
# has no F-row and no left Alt -- the CACOE sends Win and AltGr -- so
# AltGr+Left/Right get the same console keys.
::sysinit:/usr/bin/m5-key vt-keys
::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS
# USB-C/COM10 is the interactive lab console; UART0 remains the recovery fixture.
ttyGS1::askfirst:-/bin/sh
# The panel console (fbcon) gets a shell too, so the A164 keyboard dock is
# usable without a USB cable.  Same unauthenticated lab access as ttyGS1.
tty1::respawn:-/bin/sh
# Supervised daemons (#29): init restarts them if they exit.
::respawn:/usr/sbin/bootsyncd
::respawn:/usr/sbin/mdnsd -i wlan0
::restart:/sbin/init
::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot

# Drain a pending boot-drive save before the reset (#42).
::shutdown:/etc/init.d/rcK
